//! member: true
//!
//! std.bytes — the raw byte view of a string: the honesty valve D25 buys
//! by refusing `s[i]` on `str`. Stability tier: core (the API; the
//! implementation is blocked).
//!
//! **This module has no code, and that is the honest state.** `bytes` is
//! where the UTF-8 invariant is allowed to be absent — the type that says
//! "these are bytes, validity is your problem, and `to_str` is the border
//! post". Writing it needs two things this pin does not have:
//!
//! 1. **A byte value.** No expression anywhere produces the numeric value
//!    of a byte. `str` has `len` and slicing; slicing yields `str`, never a
//!    number, and a one-byte slice of a multi-byte code point does not
//!    exist to be taken (it traps `bounds`, by design). So `b[i] -> int`
//!    — the one indexing form D25 explicitly licenses — has no
//!    implementation, and neither does anything derived from it.
//! 2. **A type that can hold invalid UTF-8.** Backing `Bytes` with a `str`
//!    field would make `to_str -> str ! {utf8}` a function that can never
//!    raise, i.e. a documented lie, and would make `from_str` the only way
//!    to construct one — a byte view that cannot hold the bytes it exists
//!    for. The house rule (sc00) is that a gap in what the language can
//!    express is a finding, not a workaround invented here, so the
//!    inventory below is a reviewed CONTRACT and F-0018 carries it
//!    upstream.
//!
//! The producers are also absent, which is why this is not urgent: bytes
//! enter a program from io, FFI, and `str.bytes()` (s37 §2 and §5), and
//! Phase A has neither io nor FFI. `std.bytes` becomes writable and
//! immediately necessary in the same sprint that gives it something to
//! view.
//!
//! Reviewed inventory (9), each with its contract, exactly as it will
//! land. `Bytes` is CapCase per API-CONVENTIONS §1; the sprint contract's
//! lowercase `bytes` is the module, as here.
//!
//! ```text
//! len(b: Bytes) -> int
//!     Number of bytes. O(1). Total: no error row, no traps.
//! is_empty(b: Bytes) -> bool
//!     `len(b) == 0`. Total.
//! at(b: Bytes, i: int) -> int
//!     The byte at index `i`, 0..255. BYTE INDEXING IS LEGAL HERE — this
//!     is the whole point of the type (D25). Traps `bounds` out of range,
//!     exactly as the language's own indexing does, because a caller that
//!     could have checked `len` and did not has violated a contract (§8).
//!     The contract writes this as `b[i]`; whether index syntax is
//!     available to a std type is F-0011's builtin-versus-std question,
//!     and `at` is the spelling that does not presume the answer.
//! slice(b: Bytes, from: int, to: int) -> Bytes
//!     The bytes in `[from, to)`, zero-copy. Traps `bounds` when the range
//!     is out of order or out of range — and NEVER for splitting a code
//!     point: there are no code points here, which is the freedom this
//!     view sells. Two offsets rather than a range for the reason
//!     `str.get` gives.
//! find(b: Bytes, needle: Bytes) -> int ! {none}
//!     Index of the first occurrence of `needle`; `none` when absent. The
//!     empty needle occurs at 0. Total otherwise: no traps.
//! starts_with(b: Bytes, prefix: Bytes) -> bool
//!     Whether `b` begins with `prefix`; the empty prefix always does.
//!     Total.
//! ends_with(b: Bytes, suffix: Bytes) -> bool
//!     Whether `b` ends with `suffix`; the empty suffix always does.
//!     Total. (Note what byte-land buys: this one is trivial here and is
//!     BLOCKED over `str`, where the tail offset may not be a boundary.)
//! to_str(b: Bytes) -> str ! {utf8}
//!     `b` viewed as a string, VALIDATED: `utf8` when the bytes are not
//!     well-formed UTF-8 (overlong forms, surrogates, truncated
//!     sequences, and stray continuation bytes all miss). Zero-copy on
//!     success. This is the border post D24 demands — the one place bytes
//!     become a `str`, and the reason `str` needs no validity checks
//!     anywhere else. `utf8` is a new payload-free row tag; it earns its
//!     own noun because a caller distinguishes "not text" from "absent".
//! from_str(s: str) -> Bytes
//!     `s`'s bytes, zero-copy and infallible — a valid `str` is always
//!     valid bytes. A view, not a copy: bare-noun/`as_`-grade naming per
//!     §1, and the direction that never fails.
//! ```
//!
//! No tests ship for this module: there is nothing to run, and a test that
//! imports an itemless module is E0305 (unused import) in both
//! implementations. The ledger stays honest by having no row to lie in.
